course: dictionary
lesson: 10958
title: One word, two meanings
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Some words have more than one meaning. The dictionary numbers them 1) and 2). The words around the word, its context, tell you which meaning is being used, so read the whole sentence before you choose.
explain: Look at the capital letter. «Wallabies are smaller relatives of kangaroos» is about the animal, a small kangaroo-like creature. But «The Wallabies play rugby» has a capital W, so it means a team, not an animal.
explain: Test your meaning by putting it into the sentence. «Some wallabies brought to Britain now live wild there» only makes sense for the animal, because a rugby team cannot live wild in a field. The clue points you to the right meaning.
fact: A wallaby is an Australian animal, like a small kangaroo.
fact: A wallaby hops, and it carries its baby, called a joey, in a pouch.
fact: «Quokkas are small wallabies that live mostly on Rottnest Island» near Perth.
fact: Some wallabies were brought to Britain and New Zealand, and some now live wild there.
fact: The Wallabies, with a capital W, are the national rugby union team of Australia.
exercise: «Wallabies are smaller relatives of kangaroos.» What is a wallaby? | A bird that swims in the sea | *A small Australian animal, like a kangaroo that hops | A type of rugby ball
exercise: «The Wallabies play rugby.» What does Wallabies mean here? | A small island near Perth | *The national rugby team of Australia | A group of kangaroos
exercise: What is a joey? | A type of rugby player | *A baby wallaby, carried in its mother's pouch | A small wallaby that climbs cliffs
test: What does a wallaby carry in its pouch? | A bag of food | Water for the journey | A ball | *Its baby, called a joey
test: «Rock wallabies climb cliffs.» Which meaning of wallaby fits this sentence? | A kind of cliff | The rugby team | The island near Perth | *The animal
exam: «Some wallabies brought to Britain and New Zealand now live wild there.» Which meaning of wallaby fits, and why? | Both meanings fit equally well | The island, because islands are wild | The rugby team, because teams can travel | *The animal, because a rugby team cannot live wild
